wajo17_q1.solution:
Let \(\angle A B D=4 x\).<br>Then \(\angle A C B=x+6\) and we want \(\angle A B C=180^{\circ}-4 x\).<br>Since for a triangle, an exterior angle is the sum of the interior opposite angles,<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x &amp; =x+6+90 \\
3 x &amp; =96 \\
x &amp; =32 \\
\therefore \angle A B C=180^{\circ}-4 x &amp; =180^{\circ}-128^{\circ} \\
&amp; =52^{\circ}
\end{aligned}
$$

wajo17_q4.solution:
\(1400=8 \cdot 25 \cdot 7=2^{3} \cdot 5^{2} \cdot 7\) and \(280=2^{3} \cdot 5 \cdot 7\).<br>Hence \(y=2^{a} \cdot 5^{b} \cdot 7^{c}\), where we require<br>$$
\begin{align*}
\max \{3, a\} &amp; =3 \Longrightarrow a=0,1,2 \text { or } 3 &amp; \text{(4 possibilities)} \\
\max \{1, b\} &amp; =2 \Longrightarrow b=2 &amp; \text {(1 possibility)} \\
\max \{1, c\} &amp; =1 \Longrightarrow c=0 \text { or } 1 &amp; \text {(2 possibilities)}
\end{align*}
$$<br>Thus the number of choices for the triple ( \(a, b, c\) ) (and hence of \(y\) ) is \(4 \times 1 \times 2=8\).

wajo17_q7.solution:
$$
\begin{aligned}
x y &amp; =\left(x^{2}+2 x y+y^{2}\right)-\left(x^{2}+x y+y^{2}\right) \\
&amp; =(x+y)^{2}-\left(x^{2}+x y+y^{2}\right) \\
&amp; =11^{2}-92 \\
&amp; =121-92=29.
\end{aligned}
$$<br>Remark. The solutions for \(x, y\) are irrational, being the solutions of the quadratic equation<br>$$
\begin{aligned}
&amp; u^{2}-11 u+29=0, \\
&amp; \text { namely }\{x, y\}=\left\{\frac{1}{2}(11+\sqrt{5}), \frac{1}{2}(11-\sqrt{5})\right\}.
\end{aligned}
$$
